{"content_hash": "17994da5d5a62f4a", "version_id": 134, "article_id": 14, "level_code": "en_b2", "lang": "en", "level": 4, "level_label": "B2 中高级", "title": "Queen Camilla pays tribute to author Jeffrey Archer, dead at 86", "title_zh": "英国王后卡米拉悼念作家杰弗里·阿彻，享年86岁", "topic": "culture", "source": "bbc_culture", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm3wvq4jj64yo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/240/cpsprodpb/3d1d/live/ac47b690-c188-11f1-83c7-97e75190a976.jpg", "credit": ""}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 288, "reading_minutes": 1.4, "lead": "Jeffrey Archer, the bestselling author and former politician, has died at the age of 86. Queen Camilla remembered him as an 'irrepressible life force', while his publisher said he passed away peacefully at home.", "lead_zh": "畅销书作家、前政治家杰弗里·阿彻去世，享年86岁。卡米拉王后称他是一股“无法抑制的生命力”，而他的出版商表示他在家中安详离世。", "preview": "Queen Camilla has paid tribute to the bestselling author and former politician Jeffrey Archer, who has died at the age of 86. In a statement", "preview_zh": "卡米拉王后向畅销书作家、前政治家杰弗里·阿彻致敬，阿彻去世，享年86岁。王后在声明中表示，阿彻“并非没有缺点”，但朋友们会记得他是贯穿其人生各个篇章的一股“无法抑制的生命力”。她补充说，得知他去世的消息时她感到悲痛，而他去世时恰逢他最后一部作品出版的前夕。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 124, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@e099b16/content/audio/14/en_b2.mp3", "duration": 106.263, "size_bytes": 641376, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 328, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@e099b16/content/data/versions/134.json", "paragraphs": ["Queen Camilla has paid tribute to the bestselling author and former politician Jeffrey Archer, who has died at the age of 86. In a statement, the Queen said that Archer was 'not without flaw' but that friends would recall him as an 'irrepressible life force' through all the chapters of his life. She added that it was with sadness that she learned of his death, which came on the eve of the publication of his final work.", "Archer sold more than 300 million books during a writing career that lasted five decades. His novels, including Kane and Abel and the Clifton Chronicles, were often set in his home town of Wellington, Somerset. His publisher HarperCollins said the author died 'suddenly and peacefully at home' on Monday, between writing sessions. His last novel, Adam and Eve, was released on Tuesday.", "Born in north London in 1940, Archer moved to Somerset as a child and was elected as a Conservative MP in 1969. He served for five years before becoming deputy chairman of the party, and later entered the House of Lords in 1992. However, in 2001 he was jailed for four years for perjury and perverting the course of justice, after a court found that he had lied during his libel case against the Daily Star.", "After his release from prison in 2003, Archer returned to the House of Lords until he retired in 2024. Tributes have come from across the political world. Conservative leader Kemi Badenoch called his death 'devastating news', while former prime minister Sir John Major said we had lost 'a man who lived one of the most remarkable lives of our age'. Archer is survived by his wife Mary, their two sons and five grandchildren."], "paragraphs_zh": ["卡米拉王后向畅销书作家、前政治家杰弗里·阿彻致敬，阿彻去世，享年86岁。王后在声明中表示，阿彻“并非没有缺点”，但朋友们会记得他是贯穿其人生各个篇章的一股“无法抑制的生命力”。她补充说，得知他去世的消息时她感到悲痛，而他去世时恰逢他最后一部作品出版的前夕。", "阿彻在长达五十年的写作生涯中卖出了超过3亿本书。他的小说，包括《凯恩与亚伯》和《克利夫顿纪事》，常常以他的家乡萨默塞特郡惠灵顿为背景。他的出版商哈珀柯林斯表示，这位作家周一“在家中突然而平静地去世”，当时他正在写作间隙。他的最后一部小说《亚当与夏娃》于周二出版。", "阿彻1940年出生于伦敦北部，童年时搬到萨默塞特郡，并于1969年当选为保守党议员。他担任议员五年后成为该党副主席，后来于1992年进入上议院。然而，2001年他因作伪证和妨碍司法公正被判处四年监禁，此前法院认定他在针对《每日星报》的诽谤案中撒了谎。", "2003年出狱后，阿彻重返上议院，直到2024年退休。政界各方纷纷致以哀悼。保守党领袖凯米·巴德诺赫称他的去世是“毁灭性的消息”，而前首相约翰·梅杰爵士表示，我们失去了一位“活出了我们这个时代最非凡人生之一的人”。阿彻身后留下妻子玛丽、他们的两个儿子和五个孙辈。"], "body": "Queen Camilla has paid tribute to the bestselling author and former politician Jeffrey Archer, who has died at the age of 86. In a statement, the Queen said that Archer was 'not without flaw' but that friends would recall him as an 'irrepressible life force' through all the chapters of his life. She added that it was with sadness that she learned of his death, which came on the eve of the publication of his final work.\n\nArcher sold more than 300 million books during a writing career that lasted five decades. His novels, including Kane and Abel and the Clifton Chronicles, were often set in his home town of Wellington, Somerset. His publisher HarperCollins said the author died 'suddenly and peacefully at home' on Monday, between writing sessions. His last novel, Adam and Eve, was released on Tuesday.\n\nBorn in north London in 1940, Archer moved to Somerset as a child and was elected as a Conservative MP in 1969. He served for five years before becoming deputy chairman of the party, and later entered the House of Lords in 1992. However, in 2001 he was jailed for four years for perjury and perverting the course of justice, after a court found that he had lied during his libel case against the Daily Star.\n\nAfter his release from prison in 2003, Archer returned to the House of Lords until he retired in 2024. Tributes have come from across the political world. Conservative leader Kemi Badenoch called his death 'devastating news', while former prime minister Sir John Major said we had lost 'a man who lived one of the most remarkable lives of our age'. Archer is survived by his wife Mary, their two sons and five grandchildren.", "body_zh": "卡米拉王后向畅销书作家、前政治家杰弗里·阿彻致敬，阿彻去世，享年86岁。王后在声明中表示，阿彻“并非没有缺点”，但朋友们会记得他是贯穿其人生各个篇章的一股“无法抑制的生命力”。她补充说，得知他去世的消息时她感到悲痛，而他去世时恰逢他最后一部作品出版的前夕。\n\n阿彻在长达五十年的写作生涯中卖出了超过3亿本书。他的小说，包括《凯恩与亚伯》和《克利夫顿纪事》，常常以他的家乡萨默塞特郡惠灵顿为背景。他的出版商哈珀柯林斯表示，这位作家周一“在家中突然而平静地去世”，当时他正在写作间隙。他的最后一部小说《亚当与夏娃》于周二出版。\n\n阿彻1940年出生于伦敦北部，童年时搬到萨默塞特郡，并于1969年当选为保守党议员。他担任议员五年后成为该党副主席，后来于1992年进入上议院。然而，2001年他因作伪证和妨碍司法公正被判处四年监禁，此前法院认定他在针对《每日星报》的诽谤案中撒了谎。\n\n2003年出狱后，阿彻重返上议院，直到2024年退休。政界各方纷纷致以哀悼。保守党领袖凯米·巴德诺赫称他的去世是“毁灭性的消息”，而前首相约翰·梅杰爵士表示，我们失去了一位“活出了我们这个时代最非凡人生之一的人”。阿彻身后留下妻子玛丽、他们的两个儿子和五个孙辈。", "text": "Queen Camilla has paid tribute to the bestselling author and former politician Jeffrey Archer, who has died at the age of 86. In a statement, the Queen said that Archer was 'not without flaw' but that friends would recall him as an 'irrepressible life force' through all the chapters of his life. She added that it was with sadness that she learned of his death, which came on the eve of the publication of his final work. Archer sold more than 300 million books during a writing career that lasted five decades. His novels, including Kane and Abel and the Clifton Chronicles, were often set in his home town of Wellington, Somerset. His publisher HarperCollins said the author died 'suddenly and peacefully at home' on Monday, between writing sessions. His last novel, Adam and Eve, was released on Tuesday. Born in north London in 1940, Archer moved to Somerset as a child and was elected as a Conservative MP in 1969. He served for five years before becoming deputy chairman of the party, and later entered the House of Lords in 1992. However, in 2001 he was jailed for four years for perjury and perverting the course of justice, after a court found that he had lied during his libel case against the Daily Star. After his release from prison in 2003, Archer returned to the House of Lords until he retired in 2024. Tributes have come from across the political world. Conservative leader Kemi Badenoch called his death 'devastating news', while former prime minister Sir John Major said we had lost 'a man who lived one of the most remarkable lives of our age'. Archer is survived by his wife Mary, their two sons and five grandchildren.", "vocab": [{"word": "tribute", "pos": "noun", "zh": "悼念，致敬", "note": "pay tribute to 表示向某人致敬或悼念"}, {"word": "irrepressible", "pos": "adj", "zh": "无法抑制的，充满活力的", "note": "常用来形容人的精力或性格"}, {"word": "flaw", "pos": "noun", "zh": "缺点，瑕疵", "note": "not without flaw 意思是并非没有缺点"}, {"word": "perjury", "pos": "noun", "zh": "伪证罪", "note": "法律用语，指在法庭上撒谎"}, {"word": "pervert the course of justice", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "妨碍司法公正", "note": "法律固定搭配"}, {"word": "libel", "pos": "noun", "zh": "诽谤", "note": "指通过文字损害他人名誉"}, {"word": "peer", "pos": "noun", "zh": "贵族，上议院议员", "note": "英国上议院成员的称呼"}, {"word": "devastating", "pos": "adj", "zh": "毁灭性的，令人震惊的", "note": "常用来形容消息或影响"}, {"word": "remarkable", "pos": "adj", "zh": "非凡的，引人注目的", "note": "褒义词，表示值得注意"}, {"word": "survive", "pos": "verb", "zh": "比……活得长，身后留下", "note": "be survived by 指某人去世后还有亲属在世"}, {"word": "elected", "pos": "verb", "zh": "当选", "note": "常用被动语态 be elected as"}, {"word": "release", "pos": "noun", "zh": "释放，发行", "note": "可指从监狱释放，也可指作品发行"}], "title_original": "Queen pays tribute to 'irrepressible' Jeffrey Archer", "summary_original": "The bestselling author and former politician died on Monday at the age of 86.", "body_original": "Queen pays tribute to 'irrepressible' Jeffrey Archer\nBy\nEmma Saunders\n,\nCulture reporter\nand\nHenry Moore\nPublished\n6 October 2026\nQueen Camilla has paid tribute to the bestselling author and former politician Jeffrey Archer, who has died at the age of 86.\n\"Seldom does an author get to live a life as rich, varied and colourful as any of the characters in their books, or with as many surprising plot twists,\" the Queen said in a statement.\nShe added that Archer was \"not without flaw\", but that \"friends will recall him as an irrepressible life force through all the many differing chapters of his life\".\nThe former Conservative MP was jailed for perjury in 2001 after being found guilty of lying and cheating in his 1987 libel case against the Daily Star.\nThe Queen said he was \"an exception to the end\" and that it was \"with sadness that I learned of his death yesterday, on the eve of publication of his final work and so soon after appearing at my Queen's Reading Room Festival\".\nArcher was a supporter of the Queen's Reading Room charity.\n\"My sympathies go to Mary and the family at this time of sorrow. I hope it may be some consolation for them to know what pleasure Jeffrey brought to me and to so many millions of readers through the pages of his works.\n\"Wherever the next page turns to, I trust there will be champagne and Shepherd's Pie in abundance. Camilla R.\"\nArcher's parties were known for serving champagne and shepherd's pie to guests.\nFalse alibi\nArcher sold more than 300 million books in his five-decade writing career, including the Clifton Chronicles and Kane and Abel, and often set the novels in his home town of Wellington, Somerset.\nIn a statement, Archer's publisher HarperCollins said the author died \"suddenly and peacefully at home\" on Monday between writing sessions.\nArcher was born in north London in 1940 but soon moved to Weston-super-Mare in Somerset and in 1969, Archer was elected MP for the Lincolnshire constituency of Louth.\nHe served as an MP for five years before becoming Conservative Party deputy chairman.\nHe moved to the House of Lords in 1992 but was jailed for four years for perjury and perverting the course of justice in 2001.\nHe was found to have lied in libel action he had brought against the Daily Star newspaper, in which he was awarded £500,000 in damages.\nThe Star had alleged Archer had slept with a sex worker.\nThe peer was found by the court to have perverted the course of justice by asking a former friend to give him a false alibi and producing fake diary entries to back up his story.\nHe was also found to have perjured himself on oath during the libel trial.\nHe was released from jail in 2003 and returned to the House of Lords until he retired in 2024.\nConservative Party leader Kemi Badenoch described Archer's death as \"devastating news\".\n\"We will miss him very much,\" she told BBC Breakfast on Tuesday, adding that he had texted her earlier on Monday.\nBadenoch said he was \"very much loved by the Conservative family\", adding: \"I'm glad that I saw him recently.\"\nSpeaking about what she described as \"other parts of his life\", she said: \"He did serve his time, he made amends, and he wanted to do a lot of good in the world.\"\nFormer Conservative prime minister Sir John Major said \"we have lost a man who lived one of the most remarkable lives of our age\", and that he will be \"much missed – and long remembered\".\nArcher's last novel, Adam and Eve, was released on Tuesday.\nBroadcaster Gyles Brandreth told BBC Breakfast that he was with Archer on Sunday. \"I mean, literally 72 hours ago, doing a show in the West End of London celebrating this book, the publication, and he was in great form.\n\"He didn't really acknowledge that he'd changed much since being in prison, but I think he became a nicer person in some ways, a gentler, a milder person after the prison experience.\"\nFormer Conservative Party leader Iain Duncan Smith described Archer as \"a remarkable man who did everything at full bore\", adding: \"There were simply no half measures with him.\"\nOther former ministers also paid tribute. Sajid Javid said Archer had been a \"dear friend\", while Sir Jeremy Hunt said Archer had \"showed enormous kindness with his advice about how to become a writer\".\nJames Cleverly said the author and politician had been \"a huge personality and fantastic storyteller\", and that he had been very supportive when his wife Suzie was recovering from cancer.\nReform UK member and former Conservative minister Nadim Zahawi, wrote: \"Jeffrey Archer was a dear friend and an extraordinary human being. His gift for storytelling was matched by his wit, warmth and boundless energy. Life was never dull in Jeffrey's company.\"\nAuthor and former politician Jeffrey Archer dies aged 86\nPublished\n15 hours ago\nJeffrey Archer: bestselling novelist whose political career ended in scandal\nPublished\n1 day ago\nArcher 'nostalgic' about Somerset before death\nPublished\n14 hours ago\nLast week, Archer returned to his former school to promote his last book.\n\"It's wonderful to come back. What I got a real thrill about when I was walking towards the library is seeing the young boys and girls and thinking, 'that's the next generation',\" Archer said at Wellington School.\nArcher had been due to attend the Cheltenham Literature Festival on Saturday to discuss his final novel.\nHe and his wife Mary had celebrated their 60th wedding anniversary this year, HarperCollins said.\nThe couple have two sons and five grandchildren.\nRelated topics\nQueen Camilla\nUK Royal Family\nLiterature\nBooks", "fetched_at": "2026-10-07T00:55:38.692252", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Queen", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.45, "sm": 100, "em": 449, "cs": 0, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "Camilla", "s": 0.463, "e": 0.863, "sm": 462, "em": 862, "cs": 6, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "has", "s": 0.875, "e": 1.062, "sm": 875, "em": 1062, "cs": 14, "ce": 17, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "paid", "s": 1.075, "e": 1.35, "sm": 1075, "em": 1350, "cs": 18, "ce": 22, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "tribute", "s": 1.363, "e": 1.75, "sm": 1362, "em": 1750, "cs": 23, "ce": 30, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "to", "s": 1.762, "e": 1.85, "sm": 1762, "em": 1849, "cs": 31, "ce": 33, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "the", "s": 1.863, "e": 1.975, "sm": 1862, "em": 1975, "cs": 34, "ce": 37, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "bestselling", "s": 1.988, "e": 2.675, "sm": 1987, "em": 2675, "cs": 38, "ce": 49, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "author", "s": 2.688, "e": 3.112, "sm": 2687, "em": 3112, "cs": 50, "ce": 56, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "and", "s": 3.125, "e": 3.237, "sm": 3125, "em": 3237, "cs": 57, "ce": 60, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "former", "s": 3.25, "e": 3.625, "sm": 3250, "em": 3625, "cs": 61, "ce": 67, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "politician", "s": 3.638, "e": 4.288, "sm": 3637, "em": 4287, "cs": 68, "ce": 78, "si": 0}, {"i": 12, "w": "Jeffrey", "s": 4.3, "e": 4.65, "sm": 4300, "em": 4649, "cs": 79, "ce": 86, "si": 0}, {"i": 13, "w": "Archer", "s": 4.662, "e": 5.112, "sm": 4662, "em": 5112, "cs": 87, "ce": 93, "si": 0}, {"i": 14, "w